  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 307 AM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nOvercast skies are occurring early this morning ahead of approaching \nlow pressure. A deep trough is currently moving through the \nIntermountain West early this morning, which will be propagating a \nsfc low into the central CONUS and Upper Midwest early this week. \nSimilar to the previous forecast update, QPF is expected to be \nanomalously high, exceeding the 90th percentile of climatology. \nExpect widespread rain to move across the CWA today as the \ndeformation zone of the large low pressure moves through. While \nprecip will mostly be moderate rain, there will be a few embedded \nrumbles of thunder likely. \n\nRainfall rates are anticipated to lessen tonight through Tuesday \nmorning as the dry conveyor belt brings a shot of drier air into the \nCWA. While rain is unlikely to end completely across the region, \ncoverage will go from widespread to much more scattered during this \ntime period. Redevelopment of more widespread rain is forecast to \noccur Tuesday afternoon through Wednesday night as a shortwave dives \nsouth from northern Manitoba and causes the deep trough to shift \ninto the Upper Great Lakes region. As the deep trough moves farther \neast, this will allow for additional Gulf moisture advection. \n\nOne major change made in the forecast update this morning is an \noverall increase in total QPF amounts. Run-to-run model comparisons \nmaintain higher amounts of QPF in the 1-2\" range and the latest NBM \nprobability of 2\" or greater of rainfall in north-central MN and the \nNorth Shore terrain ridge are exceeding an 80% chance. These high \nprobabilities are also reflected in total QPF from the REFS, which \nfurther increases confidence in higher rainfall amounts. Locally \nhigher amounts are certainly possible, as the 75th percentile of QPF \nfrom the REFS in north-central MN shows the potential for 3+\" by \nearly Wednesday morning. With D1 to D4 drought persisting across the \nCWA, this widespread and soaking rain will certainly be welcome.  \n\nLight rain will gradually taper off from west to east Wednesday \nnight through Thursday as the center of the low pressure system \nbriefly stalls over the Northland and then slowly departs \ndownstream. As this low pressure departs the region, cooler air \nfilters in, with high temps being noticeably cooler in the \n40s on Thursday.\n\nHigh pressure builds into the CWA starting late Thursday, bringing \nan end to the wet pattern. Drier conditions will prevail for the end \nof the week, with some breaks in the clouds occurring on Friday into \nthe weekend. While skies are still favored to see at least partly \ncloudy skies, there will be more sunshine than what we will be \nseeing early this week. The cooler temps that arrive on Thursday \npersist on Friday through the weekend with near normal highs in the \n40s and overnight lows dropping into the upper 20s to lower 30s.",
